<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irredentism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as irredentist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Irredentism is a political movement seeking to annex territories inhabited by people of the same ethnicity or regarded as historically belonging to a nation. After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, its successor states left significant minorities across new borders. This fuelled irredentist politics in the interwar period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irredentism.jpg" alt="Irredentism" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brunodambrosio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegioniIrredenteItalia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term was coined from the Italian phrase Italia irredenta, meaning unredeemed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It referred to territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> that was mostly or partly inhabited by ethnic Italians, in particular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> but also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, during the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. An Italian movement after 1878 claimed parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the Austro-Hungarian Empire.</p><p>Irredentism is tied to the rise of modern nationalism and the nation-state, which are often linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> of 1789, and it has been an influential force in world politics since the mid-nineteenth century. Some scholars argue that similar phenomena existed earlier, for example in the claim that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> aimed to liberate fellow Christians and redeem the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, but most see it as a recent development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Irredentism is the desire of one state to annex territory of another, either because its population is ethnically similar to that of the parent state or because the territory once formed part of it. There is no consensus on its exact definition. Political theorists Naomi Chazan and Donald L. Horowitz distinguish two types: a state seeking to annex territory of a neighbour, as with Nazi Germany's claim on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and a minority spread across several countries seeking to unite in a new state, as in the proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>. Not all theorists accept the second type.</p><p>It differs from secession, in which a territory breaks away to form an independent state, and from revanchism, which seeks territory in revenge for an earlier grievance. The word revanchism comes from the French revanche and was first used after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> for nationalists who wished to reclaim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Among the cases often discussed are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megali-idea">Megali Idea</a>, the Greek nationalist project that shaped Greek history from the War of Independence in the 1820s through the Balkan Wars and began to fade after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a> of 1919 to 1922; the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> in 1938, ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> in an attempt to prevent a major war; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1977; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s invasion of the Falkland Islands in 1982.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">International law</a> is for the most part hostile to irredentism: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a> calls for respect for established borders, as do the Organization of African Unity, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act">Helsinki Final Act</a>. Irredentist movements often fail, and peaceful settlements usually bring mutual recognition of existing borders rather than territorial change.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irredentism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
